A||N||1||DaveL||Coolant Temp Sensor||||4||
Z||000000||DaveL||03-08-2000||03:24 PM||||Can an intermittent short in the circuit damage the coolant temperature sensor on an 87 SE 2.5 ? This is the one on the thermostat housing which goes to the ECM. I am having trouble with an annoying code 14 that keeps staying on.||||reg||4
Z||000001||theogre||03-08-2000||06:30 PM||||The coolant temp and manifold air temp sensors are simple thermistors. Shorting them usually won't hurt them. It can however damage the ECM, especially if the short is to a 12 volt line. These sensors run on regulated 5 volts as I recall. ((I believe that if you short the 5V supply to ground and fry it that both the coolant and manifold temp sensors go out. I don't however have schematics for this ECM to verify it.))<P>If you replace the coolant temp sensor get it from borg/warner (wrenchhead has them). Borg gives you the connector for the sensor.<P>Repare connectors for either of the sensors I mentioned above <B>MUST</B> be soldered. You cannot just crimp or twist them together. The same goes for spliced connections anyplace else along those wires.||||reg||1
